< A common way to improve language-model answers is to sample multiple responses and aggregate them. Self-consistency uses majority voting over sampled reasoning paths \cite{wang2022self}, while later work studies aggregation across multiple models and the effect of increasing the number of agents \cite{li2024agents}. More general methods assign different weights to agents using measured reliability or higher-order agreement patterns \cite{ai2025majority}. Other approaches combine or fuse whole responses rather than selecting among discrete answers \cite{jiang2023llm,chen2023universal}. Recent theory also studies the limits of aggregation policies that must return one of the answers produced by the agents \cite{ananthakrishnan2026power}. These methods are most natural when agents choose among shared alternatives or when whole responses are treated as the unit of aggregation. We instead study open-ended answers after decomposing them into smaller claims.
---
> A common way to improve language-model answers is to sample multiple responses and aggregate them. Self-consistency uses majority voting over sampled reasoning paths \citep{wang2022self}, while later work studies aggregation across multiple models and the effect of increasing the number of agents \citep{li2024agents}. More general methods assign different weights to agents using measured reliability or higher-order agreement patterns \citep{ai2025majority}. Other approaches combine or fuse whole responses rather than selecting among discrete answers \citep{jiang2023llm,chen2023universal}. Recent theory also studies the limits of aggregation policies that must return one of the answers produced by the agents \citep{ananthakrishnan2026power}. These methods are most natural when agents choose among shared alternatives or when whole responses are treated as the unit of aggregation. We instead study open-ended answers after decomposing them into smaller claims.
